# Sandbox relay (spec §6) — bridges the worker's outbound Attach stream to in-cluster/VM
# SandboxExec calls and mirrors connected workers into the Redis pool.

# SH_RELAY_PORT is the relay's own BIND port (packages/sandbox-relay/src/main.ts defaults it
# to 8443 if unset). SH_RELAY_ADDR in supervisor.env.example is the harness's DIAL address
# (harness/src/select-sandbox.ts) for the same relay. These describe the same wire and MUST
# agree on the port -- they are read by different processes out of different files, and
# nothing checks them against each other, so a change to one without the other silently
# breaks the harness -> relay connection.
SH_RELAY_PORT=9443

# Same Redis the supervisor uses (RedisRecordStore's own default, harness/src/pool-records.ts).
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379

# Token validation is fail-closed (makeDefaultValidateToken in
# packages/sandbox-relay/src/main.ts): with nothing set, every attach is rejected, including a
# tokenless one. Set this to a shared secret matching the worker's SANDBOX_TOKEN before any
# sandbox can attach -- until then, attaches fail closed, not open.
#
# Left commented out, not empty, unlike SH_TURNS_PER_WORKER= above in the supervisor template:
# readConfig throws on a blank SH_TURNS_PER_WORKER, so empty is a loud failure there, whereas
# an empty SH_RELAY_TOKEN= here would be silently accepted as the literal empty string --
# which is a valid (and useless) token, not a missing one.
#SH_RELAY_TOKEN=
